# UI Test Queries

Challenge UI tests must stay implementation-flexible. Do not require a specific label/`htmlFor`/`aria-labelledby` wiring pattern.

## Query priority

1. **Accessible roles** — `getByRole` / `findByRole` with an accessible name (`aria-label`, associated `<label>`, button text, heading text, etc.)
2. **`data-testid`** — only when there is no stable role (e.g. numeric summary tiles)

```tsx
// ✅ Preferred
screen.getByRole("combobox", { name: /status filter/i });
screen.getByRole("textbox", { name: /notes/i });
screen.getByRole("button", { name: /claim callback/i });

// ✅ OK when no role fits
screen.getByTestId("summary-open");

// ❌ Avoid — couples tests to one labeling style
screen.getByLabelText("Status filter");
```

## Authoring implications

- Name controls for assistive tech somehow (`aria-label` **or** a correctly associated `<label>`). Tests only care that the role + name resolve.
- Prefer stable accessible names in task copy (e.g. "Status filter", "Notes") so exercise UIs can pass without copying solution markup.
- Task titles must name exactly what the test asserts — one behavior per task.
- For the same control, add a simple “basic behavior works” test before adding a race-condition or stale-response test.
- Do not introduce `data-testid` for ordinary buttons, inputs, or selects that already have roles.
- Use `data-testid` for non-interactive display fields when free-text matching would couple to copy (e.g. callback list status/assignee tiles).
